WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:01.040 --> 00:00:06.160
One specific use of the os module, and important&nbsp;
enough to warrant its own exam objective,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:13.600
is the os.path submodule. Among its many uses,&nbsp;
os.path is used to see if a file exists in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:20.480
specific directory, as we are about to witness&nbsp;
with the following code example to analyze. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:25.240
code example may look familiar, as we used it&nbsp;
to see if a file did not exist, and then we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.240 --> 00:00:33.160
created a file if it did not exist and read it&nbsp;
if it did exist. In this version of the code,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.160 --> 00:00:41.320
this 613-ospath file, notice the import statement&nbsp;
on line 1. This import allows for any os module&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.320 --> 00:00:49.600
to be used in this code. Since this code focuses&nbsp;
solely on file paths, it's good practice to limit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:56.400
the import to only the os.path submodule, avoiding&nbsp;
wildcard imports that could clutter the namespace,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.400 --> 00:01:01.120
which is the set of names available in your&nbsp;
script for referencing objects. I will make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:09.120
that change on line 1. When we import a library,&nbsp;
we should restrict the import to what is needed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:14.600
so that one does not use a library module that&nbsp;
is not needed in the code and could disrupt the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:23.920
original intent of the code. In addition to&nbsp;
checking to see if a file exists, we can use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:31.240
os.path to see if a path exists to a directory and&nbsp;
possibly files within the directory. On line 15,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.240 --> 00:01:44.480
I will start a new variable, file_path, and set&nbsp;
it to an os.path.join and then, in parentheses,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:52.840
a folder and a file. Join combines a folder,&nbsp;
subfolders, and a file into a single entity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.840 --> 00:01:58.880
that can then be checked for existence. It&nbsp;
does this in an OS-agnostic manner, enabling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:04.960
cross-platform code that works on any system&nbsp;
compatible with Python. On the next line, I will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.960 --> 00:02:11.760
add an if statement to see if this path on line&nbsp;
15 is a legitimate file path. We are simulating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:17.680
if a configuration file for a game has already&nbsp;
been created in a specific folder. I will add&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.680 --> 00:02:27.120
a print statement on line 17 to confirm that the&nbsp;
file exists. I will run the code. We do not get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:34.840
a message that the file exists, so we would need&nbsp;
to create that file in a game sequence. Remember,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.840 --> 00:02:41.920
in addition to checking for file existence, you&nbsp;
can also use os.path to verify if a directory&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.920 --> 00:02:49.400
exists. This is particularly useful when your&nbsp;
application requires certain directory structures.

